  "tldr": "For a known acyclic ADMG with mixed continuous and categorical variables, the proposal replaces an uncheckable neural extremum by a finite response-label outer sieve whose two-sided lifting error is explicit and linear in the mesh. An exact likelihood-level multinomial region for a prespecified finite observational code is propagated through this sieve, yielding finite-sample uniform coverage of the entire restricted causal identified set. Rational interval branch-and-bound certificates separate sampling width, causal endpoint instability, sieve error, and global-optimization error, while graph-compatible rational ReLU SCMs provide feasible inner witnesses.",
  "project_justification": {
    "gap": "Tan, Blanchet, and Syrgkanis establish mixed-variable neural approximation and asymptotic consistency but explicitly leave finite-sample guarantees under latent confounding open. Zhang, Tian, and Bareinboim and Duarte et al. obtain canonical response-function optimization for finite observed domains, but neither gives a quantitative outer canonicalization for continuous districts. Exact multinomial regions and exact optimization certificates have not been composed with this causal compatibility map.",
    "niche": "A finite observational code makes exact sampling coverage possible, but it does not by itself control the approximation and numerical errors created by mixed-variable latent SCM optimization. The missing mathematical object is a two-sided response-label lifting that preserves the coded observational law and approximates the causal query uniformly.",
